    {'para': '**215. Causative.** The chief points to be noticed in the formation of causative-stems have been given already (in Less. VII); some additional ones follow.'},
    {'para': '**216.** Most roots in ⟨ā⟩ and ⟨ṛ⟩ add ⟨p⟩ before the conjugation-sign; thus, ⟨dāpayati⟩ from 1 ⟨dā⟩; ⟨dhāpayati⟩; ⟨gāpayati⟩; ⟨arpayati⟩ from ⟨ṛ⟩. ⟨śrā⟩ makes ⟨śrapayati⟩; ⟨jñā⟩ and ⟨snā⟩, sometimes ⟨jñāpayati⟩, etc., sometimes ⟨jñapayati⟩, etc. 1 ⟨pā⟩, ‘drink’, makes ⟨pāyayati⟩ (as though from ⟨pī⟩). A few roots in ⟨i⟩ and ⟨ī⟩ take the same ⟨p⟩, with various irregularities; thus, ⟨adhyāpayati⟩ from ⟨adhi-i⟩.'},
    {'para': '**217.** Medial or initial ⟨a⟩ in a light syllable is commonly lengthened, but sometimes remains unchanged. Thus, ⟨pat⟩, ⟨pātayati⟩; ⟨kam⟩, ⟨kāmayate⟩; ⟨cam⟩, ⟨cāmayati⟩. But most roots in ⟨am⟩, and ⟨jan⟩, ⟨tvar⟩, ⟨prath⟩, ⟨vyath⟩, with other rarer ones, generally keep the ⟨a⟩ short; thus, ⟨gam⟩, ⟨gamayati⟩.'},
    {'para': '**218.** Final vowels take *vṛddhi* before ⟨aya⟩; thus, ⟨bhū⟩, ⟨bhāvayati⟩; ⟨kṛ⟩, ⟨kārayati⟩.'},
    {'para': '**219.** Some verbs of causative meaning are by formation denominatives; thus ⟨pālayati⟩, ‘protect’, called causative to 2 ⟨pā⟩; ⟨prīṇayati⟩, to ⟨prī⟩; ⟨bhīṣayati⟩, to ⟨bhī⟩; ⟨ghātayati⟩, to ⟨han⟩.'},
    {'para': '**220.** For the passive of causatives, see Less. X.'},
    {'para': '**221.** The causatives of intransitive verbs are transitive. The causatives of transitive verbs are construed sometimes (*a*) with two accusatives, sometimes (*b*) with an acc. of the object and an instrumental of the agent. Thus, “he causes the birds to eat the cakes” may be rendered either (*a*): ⟨vihagān piṇḍān khādayati⟩, or (*b*) ⟨vihagaiḥ⟩ pi° khā° (an abbreviation for ⟨vihagaiḥ piṇḍān khādayati⟩).'},
    {'para': '**222. Participles.** The general participial endings are ⟨ant⟩ (weak form ⟨at⟩) for the active, and ⟨āna⟩ for the middle. But after a tense-stem in ⟨a⟩, the active suffix is virtually ⟨nt⟩, one of the two ⟨a⟩’s being lost; and the middle suffix is ⟨māna⟩ (except ⟨āna⟩ sometimes in causative forms). Thus, ⟨bhavant⟩, ⟨tudant⟩, ⟨dīvyant⟩, ⟨corayant⟩; ⟨bhavamāna⟩, etc. For the declension of the participles in ⟨ant⟩ see below, Less. XXIII.'},
    {'para': '**223. Pronoun of the First Person.** The pronominal declension exhibits some striking peculiarities which are not easily explained. The pronoun of the first person is declined thus:'},
    {'table': {'head': ['', 'Sing.', 'Dual.', 'Plural.'], 'rows': [['N.', '⟨aham⟩', '⟨āvām⟩', '⟨vayam⟩'], ['A.', '⟨mām⟩, ⟨mā⟩', '⟨āvām⟩, ⟨nau⟩', '⟨asmān⟩, ⟨nas⟩'], ['I.', '⟨mayā⟩', '⟨āvābhyām⟩', '⟨asmābhis⟩'], ['D.', '⟨mahyam⟩, ⟨me⟩', '„ , ⟨nau⟩', '⟨asmabhyam⟩, ⟨nas⟩'], ['Ab.', '⟨mat⟩', '„', '⟨asmat⟩'], ['G.', '⟨mama⟩, ⟨me⟩', '⟨āvayos⟩, ⟨nau⟩', '⟨asmākam⟩, ⟨nas⟩'], ['L.', '⟨mayi⟩', '„', '⟨asmāsu⟩']]}},
    {'para': '**224.** The forms ⟨mā⟩, ⟨me⟩, ⟨nau⟩, ⟨nas⟩ are enclitic, and are never used at the beginning of a sentence, or before the particles ⟨ca⟩, ⟨eva⟩, ⟨vā⟩.'},
    {'para': '**225.** In pronouns of the first and second persons the plural is often used for the singular. Pronouns (and other words as well) show in Sanskrit a curious tendency to agree in form with the predicate rather than with the subject to which they refer.'},
